+## Amendment: hook handles retain their declared public types (2026-11-14, #1763)
+
+The shared handle macro carries binding metadata onto the type it defines.
+Dispatch, state actions, optimistic setters, ref objects and forwarded refs
+use the existing types attribute and declaration reader, just as React
+aliases already do. Optimistic setters name React's canonical
+Dispatch<SetStateAction<S>> type; this adds no competing type convention.
+
+A regression exports all five handle forms, checks strict TypeScript
+positive and negative callers, and verifies callback, object and ref
+identity at runtime. Ignoring the existing metadata reader is a caught
+mutation. Runtime representation is unchanged (ADR 0262 case A); no new
+Rust/JavaScript exception is introduced.
+
+Ref metadata follows the existing React version cfg facts: React 18 names
+the mutable exact-payload handle MutableRefObject<T>; React 19 names it
+RefObject<T>. The local DefinitelyTyped clone confirms that React 18's
+RefObject<T> instead has readonly current: T | null. A regression builds
+real React 18 Rust metadata and verifies the canonical alias plus strict
+mutable-payload callers. This preserves the Rust handle's API across
+supported React releases without adding another Rust ref type.
